1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,792
The eighth of March is the busiest day of Malika's year.

2
00:00:03,842 --> 00:00:08,544
At ten past six in the evening, a buyer presses pay for order 1042.

3
00:00:08,594 --> 00:00:13,488
Bekzod's server asks the bank to create a payment, and the mobile network hiccups.

4
00:00:13,538 --> 00:00:16,128
The request may have arrived, or it may not.

5
00:00:16,178 --> 00:00:20,496
The answer never comes back, and the server cannot tell which half was lost.

6
00:00:20,546 --> 00:00:26,376
In Lesson 24, every request carried a seal, and a copied request was refused.

7
00:00:26,426 --> 00:00:32,736
This lesson makes an honest second request safe: however many times it is sent, one payment comes.

8
00:00:32,786 --> 00:00:36,456
So the server does the sensible thing, and sends the request again.

9
00:00:36,506 --> 00:00:39,816
Without care, the bank now creates a second payment.

10
00:00:39,866 --> 00:00:43,152
The buyer may get two payment pages for one bouquet.

11
00:00:43,202 --> 00:00:47,832
A nervous buyer, on the busiest evening of the year, may pay both.

12
00:00:47,882 --> 00:00:52,368
Then Malika has to give money back, and she has lost a customer's trust.

13
00:00:52,418 --> 00:00:54,240
Retrying is right.

14
00:00:54,290 --> 00:00:58,080
The problem is a retry that the bank cannot recognise.

15
00:00:58,130 --> 00:01:01,560
So every create carries an idempotency key.

16
00:01:01,610 --> 00:01:06,312
It is a plain name Bekzod gives to this one attempt, such as the order number and a counter.

17
00:01:06,362 --> 00:01:11,112
The merchant chooses it, and it stays the same on every retry of that attempt.

18
00:01:11,162 --> 00:01:18,048
The bank remembers each key for each API client, with the request it came with and the answer it gave.

19
00:01:18,098 --> 00:01:19,992
Now three things can happen.

20
00:01:20,042 --> 00:01:23,280
First, the same key arrives with the same request.

21
00:01:23,330 --> 00:01:24,984
The bank creates nothing.

22
00:01:25,034 --> 00:01:29,640
It sends back the first answer again, with the same payment and the same page link.

23
00:01:29,690 --> 00:01:35,232
It marks that answer as an idempotent replay, so Bekzod's server can see it is a repeat.

24
00:01:35,282 --> 00:01:39,912
Second, the same key arrives with a different request, say a different amount.

25
00:01:39,962 --> 00:01:41,784
The bank refuses it.

26
00:01:41,834 --> 00:01:47,664
That is a different request under an old name, and guessing which one the merchant meant is never safe.

27
00:01:47,714 --> 00:01:51,216
Third, two retries arrive at the very same moment.

28
00:01:51,266 --> 00:01:54,600
Both look new, because neither has been saved yet.

29
00:01:54,650 --> 00:01:59,760
They meet at one place in the bank's records, where each key can be written only once.

30
00:01:59,810 --> 00:02:02,040
One wins and creates the payment.

31
00:02:02,090 --> 00:02:04,944
The other loses, and gets the winner's answer back.

32
00:02:04,994 --> 00:02:07,656
One detail ties this to the last lesson.

33
00:02:07,706 --> 00:02:12,864
Every retry still needs a fresh seal, with a new time and a new one-time number.

34
00:02:12,914 --> 00:02:17,808
So the server seals the request again, but keeps the same idempotency key.

35
00:02:17,858 --> 00:02:20,496
The seal stops copies made by strangers.

36
00:02:20,546 --> 00:02:23,712
The key ties together the retries made by the merchant.

37
00:02:23,762 --> 00:02:30,120
People mix these two up all the time, and they are opposites: one refuses repeats, the other welcomes them.

38
00:02:30,170 --> 00:02:32,928
Here is order 1042 again.

39
00:02:32,978 --> 00:02:35,472
The server sends create payment with its key.

40
00:02:35,522 --> 00:02:37,344
The answer is lost on the way.

41
00:02:37,394 --> 00:02:40,896
It seals the same request again and sends it with the same key.

42
00:02:40,946 --> 00:02:42,768
This time the answer arrives.

43
00:02:42,818 --> 00:02:48,600
It holds the same payment and the same page link as the first, and it is marked as a replay.

44
00:02:48,650 --> 00:02:53,856
In the merchant portal, Malika's payments list shows one payment for this order, not two.

45
00:02:53,906 --> 00:02:55,968
Then Bekzod tests the edge.

46
00:02:56,018 --> 00:02:58,608
He reuses the key with a different amount.

47
00:02:58,658 --> 00:03:03,720
The answer is a refusal that says the key was already used for another request.

48
00:03:03,770 --> 00:03:06,072
The list still shows one payment.

49
00:03:06,122 --> 00:03:10,752
I think this is the simplest rule a developer can be given, and the most valuable one.

50
00:03:10,802 --> 00:03:13,560
When in doubt, send it again with the same key.

51
00:03:13,610 --> 00:03:19,296
Without the key, every timeout forces a hard choice between a lost order and a double charge.

52
00:03:19,346 --> 00:03:25,896
With it, the buyer pays once, Malika gives nothing back, and nobody calls her angry on the eighth of March.

53
00:03:25,946 --> 00:03:28,464
Next: the page the buyer opens.
